          "note": "[Verified Departmental Reqs] Source: admission.stanford.edu/apply/transfer/credit.html ✅ Verified June 2026. [Verified Departmental Reqs] [Verified Departmental Reqs] [Verified Departmental Reqs] [Verified Departmental Reqs] [Verified Departmental Reqs] [Verified Departmental Reqs] 【검증 완료 2026-05】 출처: admission.stanford.edu/apply/transfer/credit.html (2025-10-01 업데이트) | engineering.stanford.edu/students-academics/academics (직접 확인) | bulletin.stanford.edu programs: AA-BS, BIOE-BS, CHEMENG-BS, CEE-BS(Civil & Environmental Engineering 포함), CS-BS, EE-BS, MSE-BS(Management Science & Engineering), MATSCI-BS, ME-BS, ENGR-BS(학제간 5개 subplan), IDMEN-BS | ▶ Stanford SoE '16개 정의된 학부 전공 + IDMEN' 공식 확인 | ▶ 'There are no required courses for admission to Stanford.' 공식 명시 | ▶ Energy Resources Engineering(ERE)은 Doerr School of Sustainability 소속 — School of Engineering 소속 아님.",
          "sourceFile": "Db 6-transfercheck final.xlsx",
          "confidence": "verified",
          "rawOfficialText": "Academic Preparation &amp; Transfer Credit : Stanford University --> element --> Skip to content Undergraduate Admission --> Menu Search form Search term Discover Academics Student Life --> Plan Afford Engage Apply Visit --> Quick Links News &amp; Announcements Application Requirements Tours and Programs Cost of Attendance Parents Counselors --> Admission Volunteers Gateways for... Quick Links Application Requirements Tours and Programs Cost of Attendance Parents Counselors Admission Volunteers --> Updates for applicants in response to COVID-19 &raquo; x --> Transfer Applicants Academic Preparation &amp; Transfer Credit The primary criterion for admission to Stanford is academic excellence. We seek evidence of your preparation and potential to succeed in your academic record. College Curriculum Recommendations Your college transcript should reflect that you have taken a rigorous curriculum. There are no required courses for admission to Stanford. If you have attended college for more than one year, we expect that you have begun to focus on a potential major or area of study. We cannot provide specific guidance on course selection. We encourage students to consult with their academic advisor and pursue courses that meet the degree requirements at their current institution. Most competitive students will apply to Stanford with the equivalent of two years of college-level coursework by the time they enter Stanford. However, Stanford does not have a minimum number of credits students must have to be admitted. Transfer Credit Transfer credit is reviewed and awarded by Stanford's Office of the University Registrar according to Faculty Senate policy. While Stanford has no articulation agreements with any college or institution, a course generally receives transfer credit if it meets the following conditions: It is completed at an accredited institution It is substantially similar to courses offered at Stanford It is completed with a minimum grade of &ldquo;C-&rdquo; or Pass (where &lsquo;Pass&rsquo; is equivalent to a &ldquo;C-&rdquo; or above). It does not duplicate, overlap or regress previous coursework Courses cannot have been used to satisfy secondary/high school diploma and/or graduation requirements Incoming transfer students may transfer a maximum of 90 quarter units (roughly two years of full-time college or university study) to Stanford regardless of the number of units earned at previous institutions. While transfer students are eligible to apply with more than two years of coursework from a previous institution, note that students are required to complete at least two years of full-time enrollment at Stanford in order to attain a bachelor's degree from the university. The transferability of specific courses is ultimately determined by the Registrar's Office once students are admitted. Transferability policy may change from year to year. Consult the Office of the University Registrar for current information on transfer credit . &nbsp; Frequently Asked Questions Show All Hide All What are the requirements for applying to a specific major or department? When you apply to Stanford, you apply to the university as a whole, not to a particular major, department, or school. As a result, there are no additional requirements for certain areas of study. If you have attended college for more than one year, we expect that you have begun to focus on a potential major or area of study. I’m choosing my courses. Will they transfer? We are not able to advise students on the transferability of specific courses. Please review the guidelines listed above when choosing courses. Transferability of specific courses will be determined after students are admitted. Updated on October 1, 2025 1:36 PM APPLY Admission Overview Holistic Admission Admission Statistics --> Admission Overview First-Year Applicants Requirements and Deadlines After Submitting Your Application Regular Decision and Restrictive Early Action Preparing for Stanford Academics Application and Essays Application Fee/Fee Waiver Standardized Testing Transcript, School Report and Recommendations Interview Arts Portfolio Guidelines for Homeschooled Applicants Transfer Applicants Requirements and Deadlines After Submitting Your Application Eligibility Academic Preparation &amp; Transfer Credit Application and Essays Application Fee/Fee Waiver Standardized Testing Transcripts and College Report Letters of Recommendation Arts Portfolio International Applicants Veterans and ROTC Overview Financial Aid and the Yellow Ribbon Program Selecting the Appropriate Application Plan Key Contacts Undocumented Applicants QuestBridge Programs Admission Forums, Student Programs, and Special Events Virtual Campus Visits --> Discover Stanford --> Brochures Stanford Preview and Viewbook Join the Mailing List FAQs --> Contact Us University Policies Engage with Us --> --> --> --> Stanford complies with the Jeanne Clery Act and publishes crime statistics for the most recent three-year period. View the full report . Stanford Home Maps &amp; Directions Search Stanford Emergency Info Terms of Use Privacy Copyright Trademarks Non-Discrimination Accessibility &copy; Stanford University . Stanford , California 94305 .",
